WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:10.880
We don’t know about you, but here on the
Microcosmos team, we find it hard not to smile

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:18.539
as we watch tardigrades with their cute, pitch-black
eyes and little feet ambling across the smooth

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:21.539
glass surface of the microscope slide.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:27.039
Moss piglets, water bears—whatever you call
them, they’re fascinating to watch, and

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:30.749
even more fascinating to try and understand.

00:00:30.749 --> 00:00:35.580
They are a puzzle, and we humans are the puzzler.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.940
And so we figured, sure, we’ve already focused
on tardigrades in a previous episode.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.770
But we cannot possibly leave it there.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:55.060
After all, like the microscopic world around
them, tardigrades always have more to show us.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:02.359
Our water bears are collected from ponds and
growing moss, environments that give them

00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:05.920
the water and food they need to thrive.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.930
And so, behind the scenes, we like to give
them a bit of attention, making sure that

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:16.280
they are well fed and comfortable in their
slides.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:23.740
After all, a drop of water is barely anything
to us, but to a tardigrade, a thing so tiny

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:29.560
and so good at surviving, that drop can be
everything.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:35.460
Tardigrades of course live their lives, absent
of any kind of intention to teach us anything.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.240
They don’t even know we’re here.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:45.549
But watching is a form of interaction, and
one that reveals the interface between the

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:50.360
micro, the macro, and even the cosmic.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.479
So let’s start with a part of the tardigrade
body whose weirdness we haven’t had the

00:01:55.479 --> 00:02:00.680
chance to fully appreciate here yet: their
mouths.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:07.549
The opening of the tardigrade mouth is a distinct
ring, giving way to a tube that connects to

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:15.010
the pharyngeal bulb, which helps the tardigrade
suction out the juicy contents of its meal.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:22.660
But to get to the juice, the tardigrade first
needs to pierce its target, relying on a pair

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:28.640
of stylets that poke out of the mouth ring
when it comes time to feed.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:35.380
When the tardigrade molts, they shed their
stylets and replace them with a new pair.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:41.580
Tardigrades are omnivorous, they’re open
to eating bacteria, plants, and animals.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:45.760
For some species, their meal might even be
another tardigrade.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.500
But for the species here, our samples contain
plenty of algae and plant matter for them

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:58.129
to dine on, collected from the same watery
and earthy worlds as the tardigrades these

00:02:58.129 --> 00:03:03.330
bits of organic matter keep them well fed
like a built-in buffet.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:09.519
But there are other times where bearing witness
to the microcosmos takes a little more adaptation

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:10.959
on our part.

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:19.150
Recently, we found a water bear with its half-shed
cuticle filled with eggs, still attached to

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:24.390
its body in what amounts to parenting in the
microcosmos.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:30.360
As you might imagine, we were more than a
little curious to watch these eggs develop.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:37.209
But if you keep a slide on a microscope for
too long, the water will evaporate, taking

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:43.970
with it the moisture the tardigrade needs
to exist outside of its dessicated, dormant state.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:50.129
So we kept these slides in humidity chambers
and took extra care to make sure the coverslip

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.840
wouldn’t crush the developing tardigrades.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.220
And then…we watched.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:05.060
The tardigrade though was still.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:13.200
This video is from our sixth day of observation, the only sign of life being the absence of decomposition.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:23.920
Three days later, we can see the eggs are
developing, and there are even little tardigrade

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.760
heads appearing.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:33.170
The mother still seems the same, attached
to her eggs and seemingly still alive despite

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:34.720
her complete stillness.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:43.160
When it comes time to hatch, the embryos
will use their stylets and pharyngeal pump

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:50.010
to make their way out, sucking in water to
increase the pressure on the egg from the inside

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.250
until the shell breaks.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:58.000
But…we’re still waiting for that day to
come with this tardigrade brood.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:05.430
Embryonic development in tardigrade can last
from 5 days to more than 100, varying by species

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.700
and dependent on environmental factors like
temperature.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:14.770
The thing with biology, no matter what scale
of creature you’re looking at, is that you’re

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:18.340
always on their schedule, not the other way
around.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.920
Food and birth probably seem a bit domestic
compared to some of the more recent tardigradian

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.180
exploits you may have heard about.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:40.810
Earlier in 2019, Israel sent a spacecraft
called Beresheet to the moon, taking with

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:47.150
it, among many things, a digital archive of
almost the entirety of English Wikipedia and

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:56.360
also thousands of tardigrades, dried out in
their ball-like tun form and ready for lift off.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:02.360
Seconds before Beresheet was supposed to land,
however, it lost contact with Mission control

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:10.990
and crashed instead, leaving one very obvious
question: what will happen to those thousands

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:16.600
of tardigrades, potentially spilled across
the surface of the moon.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:22.270
Our seeming penchant for sending tardigrades
into space has taught us that they’re able

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.840
to tolerate vacuums.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.660
Without liquid water, of course, the tardigrades
will remain in their dormant state.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:35.550
Maybe if someone is able to return to that
crash site decades from now, they might manage

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:40.950
to find tardigrades that survived that crash,
and there’s a chance that with a little

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:44.400
water, they could come back to life.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:52.040
We’re still working to understand all of
the tricks of tardigrade survival, how does

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:57.510
it play out in their DNA and proteins, and
why some tardigrade species seem to be better

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.310
than others.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:04.610
This is part of the fun of tardigrades and
our collective fascination with them.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:10.610
We’re all learning together here on planet
earth, like a giant science classroom, asking

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:17.310
important questions like, “Is that round
thing the mouth?” and “What does happen

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.970
when you put a tardigrade on the moon?”

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:27.510
Maybe this is our 21st century remix of Apollo,
a conversation between microcosmos and good

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:29.870
old-fashioned cosmos.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:37.050
Science is ultimately a mix of deliberate
and accidental questions, taking on many forms

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:40.350
to suit our available tools.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:44.920
Sometimes science is a detailed study, and
sometimes it’s a crash landing.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:51.270
It can be a collector of microbes and a master
of microscopes, carefully tending to samples

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.560
to meet the needs of their invisible residents.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:08:01.720
And sometimes it’s you, watching along,
making your own observations, maybe even thinking

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:07.060
of new questions that no one has ever thought to ask before.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:13.200
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.060
And thank you to all of these patrons.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:22.600
All of these wonderful people who it possible for us to take care of our microscopic friends

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:27.240
and bring you these videos once a week here on Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:30.300
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James,

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.840
check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.000
And if you want to see more from us, we've got a lot of videos up now.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.280
Check em out at youtube.com/microcosmos.

